module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.by_value_record_widens_container_slots

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data by_value_record_widens_container_slots: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "by_value_record_widens_container_slots" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "A repair that trades one allocation for a wider by-value copy lowers every allocation count its label reads and raises cost it does not read. The seed emitter fact F (a record whose every field clones without allocating is held by value instead of behind an Rc) took the state-threading specimen from 8116 to 4114 allocator operations (native-allocation-observation, n=2000) and was reported as a win. Run end to end on a representative governed arm it was a regression: gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:value-position-block-nests, prebuilt base (5ef31256) against head (5121c34a) built in one dispatch, 9 alternating runs, outputs equal, peak RSS equal (~15.6 MB): median 0.637 s at base, 0.752 s at head, and 5.644G against 7.247G instructions under callgrind (+28%).",

    "THE MECHANISM, located by callgrind rather than assumed: the whole delta was two flipped records, v2_compiler_parse LookaheadKey {Rc, Rc} (16 B by value, 8 B as an Rc) and LookaheadPrefix {Rc, Bool, Rc} (24 B), both ELEMENTS of im::Vector, whose chunks hold 64 inline slots, so a chunk allocation and every copy-on-write scale with sizeof(element): 512 B at base, ~1 KB and ~1.5 KB at head. The malloc family (libc _int_malloc 137M -> 780M, memcpy 650M -> 972M, chunk free paths) was ~1.2G of the 1.6G increase; the rest of the program matched to ~0.1%. That a chunk crossing glibc's ~1032 B tcache limit is the dominant term is an inference from that size arithmetic and the _int_malloc jump, not a measured allocation-size histogram. The refcount arithmetic (a by-value clone is one increment per Rc field, an Rc clone is one) is real but not the dominant term.",

    "THE NARROWED RULE AND WHY IT WAS NOT LANDED: the sound claim is width(R) <= width(Rc<R>), because then no slot, chunk or memcpy is ever wider than the representation it replaces. Under it about 60 of the 372 records F flipped survive, every one a single-handle newtype (an Rc around a Vec, HashMap, Node or DeclarationRef), saving one Rc allocation per construction with no measured effect on any arm; SpecCursor and SpecAcc (the specimen) are 16 B and wider and fail it. A fact whose only survivors are unmeasured newtypes has no consumer (DESIGN section 3c), so F was reverted to its base behavior rather than landed as plumbing.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: a label that counts allocator operations and bytes requested cannot judge a repair that moves a cost from allocation to copy width, and the emit+build wall judges the compiler's own build, not the changed records. Any proposal that flips a representation (Rc to by-value, boxed to inline) owes a runtime comparison of prebuilt base and head binaries on a representative workload that holds the changed records inside containers, with outputs equal, before it is called a win.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
